sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-07> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Cellular automata can emulate nearly all other system types, even computers" ;
    dct:description "Cellular automata can be set up to emulate almost every type of system considered in the book—mobile automata, Turing machines, substitution systems, sequential substitution and tag systems, register machines, arithmetic systems, logic circuits and random-access memory—and in principle a practical computer in its entirety." ;
    delphi:context "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 11, “The Notion of Computation”; section “Emulating Other Systems with Cellular Automata” (from p. 656), p. 656. Scope: cellular automata versus mobile automata, Turing machines, substitution systems, tag systems, register machines, arithmetic systems and practical computers" ;
    dct:type "mathematical" .
}

